---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import { CONTENT_PAGE_SEO, buildContentPageJsonLd, pathForMode } from '../../seoData'

const seo = CONTENT_PAGE_SEO['heic-to-pdf']
const origin = Astro.site?.origin ?? 'https://bentotools.app'
---

<BaseLayout
  title={seo.title}
  description={seo.description}
  ogTitle={seo.ogTitle}
  ogDescription={seo.ogDescription}
  path={seo.path}
  jsonLd={buildContentPageJsonLd('heic-to-pdf', origin)}
  variant="content"
  tagline={seo.tagline}
>
  <article class="content-page">
    <header class="content-page__header">
      <h1 class="content-page__title">Convert HEIC to PDF privately</h1>
      <p class="content-page__lede">
        iPhone photos often arrive as HEIC. You can turn them into a shareable
        PDF without sending the images to a convert farm. Bento Tools does the
        work in your browser — free, with no account.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="content-page__section" aria-labelledby="why-heic">
      <h2 id="why-heic" class="content-page__h2">Why HEIC shows up</h2>
      <p>
        Apple devices commonly capture stills as HEIC (or HEIF) to save space
        while keeping quality. Many email clients, Windows PCs, and forms
        still expect JPG or a multipage PDF. Converting yourself — on the
        same device — keeps travel, medical, and work photos out of someone
        else’s upload queue.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-page__section" aria-labelledby="how-private">
      <h2 id="how-private" class="content-page__h2">
        How Bento Tools keeps it private
      </h2>
      <p>
        The <a href={pathForMode('images')}>Images → PDF</a> tool runs conversion
        in the browser (including client-side HEIC handling when the format is
        supported). Your photos are not uploaded for the conversion step. You
        download the PDF when you are ready; the server never needs the file
        to build it.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the full story of what stays local and what can still use the
        network (loading the app, optional analytics, voluntary feedback
        text), see <a href="/privacy">Privacy &amp; about</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-page__section" aria-labelledby="steps">
      <h2 id="steps" class="content-page__h2">Steps: HEIC photos → one PDF</h2>
      <ol class="content-page__list content-page__list--ordered">
        <li>
          Open <a href={pathForMode('images')}>Images → PDF</a> on Bento Tools.
        </li>
        <li>
          Drop or choose HEIC files (JPG, PNG, WebP, and GIF work too if you
          are mixing formats).
        </li>
        <li>
          Reorder pages if needed, check the full-screen preview, and pick a
          page size (fit / A4 / US Letter).
        </li>
        <li>Download the PDF. It is built on this device, then saved by you.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="content-page__section" aria-labelledby="tips">
      <h2 id="tips" class="content-page__h2">Tips that avoid headaches</h2>
      <ul class="content-page__list">
        <li>
          <strong>Large photos</strong> — Bento Tools may downscale oversized
          images on export so phones do not run out of memory. That is still
          local processing.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Unsupported HEIC edge cases</strong> — some exotic variants
          fail in the browser. You’ll get a clear error rather than a silent
          cloud retry.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Order matters</strong> — arrange images before download if
          receipts or document photos need a fixed sequence.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="content-page__section" aria-labelledby="related">
      <h2 id="related" class="content-page__h2">Related tools</h2>
      <p>
        After you have a PDF, you can keep working locally — merge more files,
        export pages as images, or slim a heavy export:
      </p>
      <ul class="content-page__tools">
        <li>
          <a class="content-page__tool-link" href={pathForMode('images')}>
            Images → PDF
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="content-page__tool-link" href={pathForMode('merge')}>
            Merge PDFs
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="content-page__tool-link" href={pathForMode('extract')}>
            PDF → images
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="content-page__tool-link" href={pathForMode('slim')}>
            Slim PDF
          </a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <p class="content-page__note">
      This guide matches Bento Tools as it ships today. The product promise stays
      the same: conversion for this job runs on your device, not as an upload
      to process your photos.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
